.TH DOCKERFILE\-HARDENER 1 "2026-09" "dockerfile-hardener" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dockerfile-hardener \- rewrite a Dockerfile to best practice and show the diff
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dockerfile-hardener
.I DOCKERFILE
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Not a linter: it produces the fixed file and a unified diff you can apply.
.PP
By default the diff goes to standard output and nothing is written, so the
change can be read before it lands.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-write
Apply the changes in place.
.TP
.B \-\-explain
Explain every change.
.TP
.B \-\-fail\-on\-changes
CI mode: exit 1 if the file is not already hardened.
.TP
.B \-\-pin\-digests
Resolve
.B FROM
tags to a registry digest. Docker Hub only, and needs network access.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The file is hardened, or a diff was printed.
.TP
.B 1
With
.BR \-\-fail\-on\-changes ,
the file needed changes.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
See what would change:
.PP
.RS
.B dockerfile-hardener Dockerfile \-\-explain
.RE
.PP
Apply it:
.PP
.RS
.B dockerfile-hardener Dockerfile \-\-write
.RE
.PP
Gate a pull request:
.PP
.RS
.B dockerfile-hardener Dockerfile \-\-fail\-on\-changes
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR docker (1),
.BR hadolint (1).
Project documentation:
https://github.com/fabiocicerchia/dockerfile-hardener
